      "content": "Mainstream historical accounts are not neutral reflections; they are curated narratives that reflect the dominant political and military power structures of the time. When the state's survival is framed as the central historical necessity, it naturally becomes the dominant, accepted \"operative truth\" in official histories. The claims of displaced populations, while representing a profound lived reality, are systematically marginalized in these dominant texts because they lack the institutional apparatus to enforce their narrative against the established power. This is not a failure of their truth, but a reflection of the asymmetrical power dynamics of the conflict.",
      "source": "target",
